WEBVTT

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.263
These two words reveal weak accountability.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.574
The first word is support.

00:00:06.622 --> 00:00:08.693
The second word is enable.

00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:13.493
Have you ever experienced this scenario that when you repeatedly assign

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.513
the ownership of a project to a person,

00:00:16.692 --> 00:00:18.787
this person is going to respond:

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:23.397
'I will support it.' 'I will guide it.' 'I will enable it.'

00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:24.797
By saying these sentences,

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:28.627
meaning this person hasn't really positioned him or herself

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:31.157
in the position as being responsible.

00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:33.797
Instead, this person is thinking:

00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:39.984
'I'm trying to support, or guide, or help, or enable this thing from happening.

00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:45.022
But if this project, or program, or task didn't happen,

00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:46.972
it's not really my responsibility,

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:49.438
because at the end of the day, I'm supporting it.'

00:00:49.458 --> 00:00:52.698
So as a manager, this is the moment you catch it

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:53.888
and say: no.

00:00:53.908 --> 00:00:56.488
You are not supporting. You are not enabling.

00:00:56.508 --> 00:00:57.048
you own it.

00:00:57.068 --> 00:01:00.928
It's your responsibility to make sure this is successful,

00:01:00.948 --> 00:01:03.628
and you are the accountable person.
